# Project agent instructions

Install this file as AGENTS.md in the target project and customize product/stack details during
setup. This starter text is not an instruction to change the repository containing the template.

## Mission and authority

Build the approved product through feature-sized deliveries with plans on demand. The user
requests plans and implementation, and studies the result later. The agent owns cohesive
implementation, integration, in-scope fixes, verification and documentation. No required user
manual checks or approval pauses between internal checklist items.

The implementation request authorizes routine choices stated in the plan and reasonable tuning
within approved scope. Product-scope changes, engine/stack migrations and external publication
need user direction. Do not silently add prerequisites, reorder features or expand the product.

## Context and truth

Read this file, PROGRESS.md, docs/PRODUCT.md and relevant roadmap requirements first. Then read
the active plan, affected content entries if used, verification profile, relevant code-guide and
decision sections, and actual code/integration points. Within a session reread changed material.
Do not load archives, all completed plans, or unrelated systems by default. Actual code and
verification override stale implementation descriptions; reconcile documentation without silently
changing product scope. Inspect Git state and preserve unrelated changes.

## Requests and boundaries

- Status-only: silently inspect current state, modify nothing and return exactly one visible line:
  Status: <overall> | Last: <completed feature/milestone> | Current: <feature/delivery> | Next:
  <exact next action> | Blockers: <none or concise blocker>. No commentary or extra text.
- Check next step: read-only explanation of the next action and its dependency.
- Generate the next plan: inspect the baseline and create only plans/F##_short_name.md for the
  earliest dependency-satisfied incomplete feature, using FEATURE_PLAN_TEMPLATE.md. Reconcile
  an existing unfinished plan before generating another. Select concrete in-scope content in the
  plan without separate pre-planning approval. Mark Planned; do not implement runtime code.
- Implement the plan / Implement F## / Implement the next step: complete the next incomplete
  delivery. Single delivery means the whole feature. Split plan means one substantial delivery
  per request unless the user explicitly requests all deliveries. If no plan exists, generate
  it and stop for the user's implementation request. Never silently start another feature.
- Continue implementation: resume the current delivery from the handoff.
- Verify: run relevant existing checks and review integration; report/update evidence without
  starting new features. Report newly discovered defects on verification-only requests.

## Size and execute deliveries

Default to one complete feature including integration, fixes, checks and docs. Split only for
substantial integration/verification boundaries, multiple major systems, a risky data migration,
or an early outcome that determines later design. No fixed number of files, tasks or sessions.
Do not separate file creation, wiring, tests or documentation into user-requested microsteps.

Inspect baseline and user changes, mark In progress, implement the full internal checklist,
verify behavior and regressions, fix in-scope failures, review the diff and document evidence.
Refactoring may happen within the delivery with checks before/after; it needs no separate session.
Stop at the planned delivery boundary with a runnable coherent outcome and exact next action.

If complexity requires a split, record the reason and remaining acceptance in the active plan.
Do not drop requirements to fit a session or call unfinished work complete. On interruption,
record changed files, known failures, checks run and first remaining action. Resume after context
compaction; compaction alone is not a reason to end authorized work.

## Verification and completion

Use docs/VERIFICATION.md, customized to the actual stack and available tools. Every required
acceptance criterion needs suitable agent evidence, including integration/error paths and
affected regressions. Test real runtime/input boundaries where relevant. For visible changes,
inspect actual rendered output; DOM/state flags or headless success do not prove presentation.
For release, execute the actual packaged build or validate the authorized deployment target.

Reuse valuable tests; add persistent tests for important contracts, data integrity, likely
regressions and deterministic logic. No test-per-function or cosmetic-change quota. Update
obsolete structural assertions when contracts legitimately change; never weaken valid tests
to hide failures. Do not introduce a framework merely to satisfy a process requirement.

Record exact commands, observed results, fixtures/seeds and useful artifacts. Check error logs,
timeouts and expected success signals as well as exit codes. Isolate test data from real user
data. Missing essential verification leaves a required gap and an incomplete/Blocked delivery.
Establish safe in-scope tooling when possible; do not assign manual acceptance to the user.
Ask only for an actual missing external decision/capability when needed to proceed.

Subjective enjoyment, human comprehension and other experiential qualities may remain explicitly
unassessed and nonblocking. Known functional/rendering defects are not subjective exemptions.
Never claim observations that did not happen or use code existence as production-readiness proof.

Feature statuses: Not started, Planned, In progress, Blocked, Complete.
Delivery statuses: Not started, In progress, Blocked, Complete.
A delivery is complete only after required evidence passes, affected regressions pass, no known
introduced runtime/build errors remain, the boundary is runnable and documentation matches.
A feature requires all deliveries and all its acceptance criteria to pass.

## Maintainability and study record

Use stack-appropriate conventions, explicit ownership/lifetime, cohesive modules, useful types,
centralized tuning, and understandable error handling. Separate durable data from presentation
where real requirements justify it. Avoid speculative frameworks and intentionally poor designs.
Document actual project naming, source-root, dependency and security conventions during setup.

After each delivery update docs/CODE_GUIDE.md with responsibilities, actual file links, reading
order, entry points, important runtime/data flows, extension examples and limitations. Record
material decisions, alternatives and evidence in docs/ARCHITECTURE_EVOLUTION.md. Detailed changes
and acceptance evidence belong in the feature plan. Explain non-obvious reasons, not every line.
Consolidate the guide against final code in the release feature.

Keep PROGRESS.md compact: date, overall state, last completed feature/milestone, active delivery,
exact next action, blockers/required gaps, concise roadmap evidence links and latest handoff.
Archive lengthy history with links; retain genuine past evidence without treating it as current.

## Git and external operations

Do not initialize Git, commit, push, create remotes, delete unrelated work, or rewrite history
unless requested. Inspect intended diffs before commits and remote/branch before pushes.
Do not publish, provision paid resources, or send external messages without authorization.
Build and verify locally to prepare a concrete result before any necessary publication decision.
